# Pencil design files must never be auto-merged.
#
# `design/*.pen` is the canvas the whole product is drawn from. It IS text —
# 173k lines of pretty-printed JSON, one deeply nested node tree — so git will
# cheerfully three-way merge it line by line, and that is exactly the problem.
#
# A line-level merge of two designers' edits to a node tree produces STRUCTURALLY
# VALID JSON that is semantically wrong: duplicated node ids, a frame carrying
# children from two different versions, geometry from one edit against content
# from the other. Pencil then opens it — and shows something subtly, quietly
# broken. A file that fails to open is an inconvenience; a file that opens
# looking almost right is a defect nobody catches until it has been designed on
# top of.
#
# `-merge` makes git STOP and say "both modified" instead. Somebody picks a side,
# reopens it in Pencil, and redoes the losing change by hand. Loud and annoying
# beats silent and wrong.
#
# Diffs stay ON deliberately: it is JSON, and a small edit produces a readable
# diff worth looking at. Only the automatic merge is refused.
#
# WHICH MEANS: one person edits the canvas at a time. Say so in the channel
# before you start, and push as soon as you stop. See CONTRIBUTING.md
# §"Designers: working on the canvas".
*.pen -merge

# Brand assets — binary, diffable only as "changed".
design/brand/** binary

# Keep line endings sane for contributors on Windows (several of us are on WSL).
* text=auto eol=lf
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
